 CellLabel->"In[1]:=",ExpressionUUID->"4163d6a9-1b12-4a96-a78b-0684afebe2fd"],

Cell["2. The idea: read everything, then write", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"e11486dd-636c-431a-aee0-27a43c7841d2"],

Cell["\<\
A sentence and its translation rarely have the same length, and word i of one \
is not word i of the other. So the paper uses two networks. The encoder reads \
the source one word at a time and ends in a fixed-size vector v, the \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]thought vector\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. The decoder \
starts from v and writes the target one word at a time, feeding each word it \
writes back in as its next input, until it writes <EOS>.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ad55eb7c-c6da-4ee8-a192-d9743e74a47d"],

Cell["p(y\:2081, \[Ellipsis], y_T\[Prime] | x\:2081, \[Ellipsis], x_T) = \
\[Product]\:209c p(y\:209c | v, y\:2081, \[Ellipsis], y\:209c\:208b\:2081)", \
"Text",
 TextAlignment->Center,
 FontFamily->"Times",
 FontSize->15,
 FontSlant->"Italic",ExpressionUUID->"465a7f25-9bbe-4944-98dd-282e84480486"],

Cell["\<\
Three choices made it work (Section 2 of the paper): two different LSTMs for \
reading and writing, four layers deep, and the source fed in reverse (\
\[Section]6).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"49e72cb2-614d-4f83-ac00-8782f76eecf1"],

